uthor_id":"A5088262294","first_year":2025,"h_index":8,"in_atlas_index":true,"last_year":2025,"name":"Chihyun Kim","plastic_citations":3,"plastic_works_count":1,"topics":[],"works_at_institution":1,"years":{"2025":1}}],"institution":{"atlas_type":"nonprofit/NGO","author_count":9,"ca_flag":true,"city":"Los Angeles","domain_tags":["air","analytical_methods","fate_transport_and_sorption","human_exposure_and_health_risk","matrix:air","matrix:food_and_drink","occurrence_and_monitoring","wastewater_and_treatment"],"inst_id":"I4210097936","lat":34.03628158569336,"lon":-118.44550323486328,"name":"Terasaki Foundation","region":"California","ror":"012381002","scope":"california","work_count":9},"schema_version":"s3.1","works":[{"abstract":"The plastic pieces of synthetic polymers, which were previously regarded as primary pollutants of the environment, are increasingly being discovered as internal pollutants of the human body. This review provides a comprehensive overview of the available evidence on human exposure, tissue distribution, and associated biological effects of micro- and nanoplastics. Ingesting contaminated food and water is the major exposure pathway, with inhalation and dermal contact being secondary routes. Various organ systems have been identified as containing polymer particles through the use of advanced analytical methods, including blood, liver, lungs, placenta, breast milk, and brain tissue. Experimental animal studies suggest associations with tissue injury, metabolic illness, and neurotoxicity. Polyethylene, polypropylene, polystyrene, and polyethylene terephthalate are the most frequently found polymers in human samples. New clinical findings indicate potential health implications, though current human evidence remains largely associative rather than causal: a cardiovascular study observed more than a two-fold rise in mortality among patients with polymer-containing arterial plaques, and recent evidence demonstrates over-accumulation of polymers in brain tissue, raising questions about neuroinflammatory processes. Detection technologies have advanced substantially, with deep learning-based polymer classification achieving 95\u201399% accuracy and ultrasensitive electrochemical and surface plasmon resonance biosensors reaching detection limits approaching 10\u221211 M. Despite these advances, critical issues remain, including lack of standardized analytical procedures, absence of chronic exposure models for humans, and insufficient longitudinal epidemiological data. To address these gaps, physiologically relevant experimental systems including organoids and organ-on-chip platforms will be required, in addition to well-designed prospective cohort studies.","author_ids":["A5003698779","A5020396513","A5064097008","A5117754746"],"author_names":["Ramesh Ganpisetti","Sanjay Giridharan","Mehmet R. Dokmeci","Radhika Chandankere"],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":false,"cited_by_count":4,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3390/microplastics5030131","domain_tags":["air","human_exposure_and_health_risk","occurrence_and_monitoring"],"facet_tags":["matrix:air","matrix:food_and_drink"],"institution_ids":["I144614875","I4210097936","I55732556"],"is_oa":true,"journal":"Microplastics","last_updated":"2026-10-14T16:39:01.820549","license":"cc-by","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"gold","oa_url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/microplastics5030131","openalex_topics":["T10419","T10686","T10753"],"plastic_related":true,"plastic_tier":"core","primary_topic":"T10753","pub_date":"2026-06-23","study_type":"article","title":"Microplastics and Nanoplastics in Human Health: From Environmental Contaminants to Internal Pollutants\u2014A Comprehensive Review of Exposure, Bioaccumulation, Toxicity Mechanisms, and Emerging Detection Technologies","type":"article","work_id":"W7165644718","year":2026},{"abstract":"Plastic biodegradation offers a sustainable complementary strategy for plastic waste management via biological depolymerization of synthetic polymers. Insects have demonstrated the ability to degrade various plastics through the synergistic action of their digestive system and intestinal microbiota. Additionally, numerous microbial strains have been identified with enzymatic capabilities to break down plastic polymers. Despite advances in this field, a major barrier to scientific progress is the lack of standardization in experimental methodologies. Variability in biodegradation agents, types of plastic, environmental conditions, and analytical approaches hinder the reproducibility, comparability, and practical application of results. Although several international standards exist for specific environments, their scope is limited and not universally applicable. This review highlights the urgent need for standardized, reproducible, and globally accepted protocols. It also emphasizes collaboration, methodological rigor, and technological innovation as central to the future of plastic biodegradation research. Clear and reliable biodegradability criteria are fundamental to scientific integrity, public trust, policymaking, and responsible innovation. The article concludes with recommendations to improve standardization across methodological, reporting, and collaborative dimensions. \u2022 Inconsistent definitions of biodegradation lead to systematic overestimation of degradation efficiency \u2022 Physical fragmentation and surface oxidation are frequently misinterpreted as mineralization \u2022 Polymer properties (e.g., crystallinity and Tg) critically influence enzymatic degradation rates \u2022 Inadequate reporting of pre-treatment and material characterization impairs data comparability \u2022 A multi-level framework is proposed to harmonize experimental design, validation metrics, and reporting practices","author_ids":["A5012876343","A5012957048","A5014979148","A5025648479","A5031993549","A5034729579","A5059789817","A5067656771","A5076759824","A5077278637","A5080633438","A5084527213","A5086061321","A5117063879"],"auth